# The docs toolchain, as a human edits it. requirements.txt is GENERATED from this file:
# every transitive dependency pinned, with the sha256 of every file PyPI serves for it, so
# `pip install -r requirements.txt` refuses a package that changed under the same version.
# After editing this file, regenerate (Linux, macOS or Windows alike; the lock is universal):
#
#   uv pip compile requirements.in --universal --python-version 3.12 --generate-hashes -o requirements.txt
#
# The security workflow (.github/workflows/security.yml) scans requirements.txt with Trivy,
# so a transitive package is audited as well as the ones named here.
mkdocs==1.6.1
# 9.5.44 -> 9.7.7 (2026-09-25): CVE-2026-73295 (DOM XSS, MEDIUM), fixed in 9.7.7. It also
# lifts 9.5's `pymdown-extensions~=10.2` cap, which was holding the line below on 10.x.
# 9.7 prints a red "MkDocs 2.0" notice on stderr at every build: informational only (not a
# log warning, --strict ignores it); 9.7 requires mkdocs<2 and mkdocs is pinned above.
mkdocs-material==9.7.7
# A TRANSITIVE dependency of mkdocs-material, pinned here because 9.7 only asks for >=10.2:
# unpinned, the lock would jump to whatever major is newest the day it is regenerated.
# 10.21.3 -> 11.0.2 (2026-09-25): CVE-2026-61632 (fixed in 11.0) and CVE-2026-67422
# (ReDoS, HIGH, fixed in 11.0.1). 11.0's only BREAK is the B64 extension (not used here).
# Not 12.x: 12.0 rewrites BetterEm / Tilde / Caret / Mark parsing, a separate change.
pymdown-extensions==11.0.2
mkdocs-static-i18n==1.2.3
mkdocs-with-pdf==0.9.3
# 11.0.0 -> 12.3.0 (2026-09-25): 13 HIGH advisories (heap writes / DoS on crafted images).
# WeasyPrint 70 is itself tested against Pillow >= 12.1 (its `test` extra).
Pillow==12.3.0
# WeasyPrint is a TRANSITIVE dependency of mkdocs-with-pdf and was the only thing here left
# unpinned, so the PDF renderer changed under the build while everything else stayed put.
# It matters because the apt list in .github/workflows/docs.yml has to match what THIS
# version dlopens (see weasyprint/text/ffi.py): v53 dropped cairo, v57 added harfbuzz-subset.
# Pin them together, or the two drift apart again.
# 69.0 -> 70.0 (2026-09-25): CVE-2026-55073, fixed in 70.0. ffi.py in 70 loads the same
# libraries plus an OPTIONAL libharfbuzz-vector (allow_fail; HarfBuzz 13+, only used to
# draw COLRv1 emoji fonts; Noto Color Emoji is a bitmap font), so the apt list is unchanged.
# mkdocs-with-pdf 0.9.3 only calls HTML(string=).render().write_pdf(), urls.path2url /
# iri_to_uri / url_is_absolute / urljoin and logger.LOGGER: all still there in 70.
# 70 regressed line breaking: a paragraph could print one word per line just before an
# inline <code> (3 paragraphs in the French book). pdf_event_hook.py patches that one
# branch back to 69's result, gated on 70.x; a bump must re-check the book (see the hook).
weasyprint==70.0
# Reading the built PDF back, so a render that silently drops 90% of the book fails the
# build instead of shipping. See tools/check_pdf.py.
# 5.1.0 -> 6.16.2 (2026-09-25): 2 HIGH DoS advisories. check_pdf.py only uses PdfReader,
# .pages and extract_text(); both versions give the same verdicts on a 301-page build.
pypdf==6.16.2
